1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,680
This is Doing and
Feeling, the long version.

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:05,560
Episode three: Intelligence, Formally.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:11,050
In two thousand six, the computer scientist
Marcus Hutter put up prize money for an

4
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:16,010
odd challenge: compress a hundred
megabytes of English Wikipedia as small as

5
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:21,290
possible. The program that decompresses
it counts toward the total size, so you

6
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:24,450
can't cheat by hiding the
text inside the decompressor.

7
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,870
Since two thousand twenty, the challenge
has used a full gigabyte, with a prize fund

8
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:34,600
of up to half a million euros, and the
record fell three times in two thousand

9
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:36,800
twenty six, most recently in July.

10
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:40,460
Why would zipping Wikipedia have
anything to do with intelligence?

11
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:45,510
The answer connects information theory,
the foundations of statistics, a

12
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:50,170
mathematical definition of intelligence,
and the training objective of every large

13
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:55,440
language model. Last episode, intelligence
took many forms in many bodies.

14
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:00,700
This one asks whether there's a definition
that doesn't depend on any species, body

15
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:02,900
or test. Here's the argument.

16
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,950
The best formal definitions tie intelligence
to prediction and compression across

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,190
many environments. But every definition
builds in assumptions, about what counts as

18
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:17,560
simple, or about which tasks matter,
and no learner is best at everything.

19
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,790
That is why measuring machine intelligence
is so contested, and why a score can

20
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:25,600
change dramatically depending
on how a model is run.

21
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:32,090
Start with Alan Turing. In nineteen fifty,
he proposed replacing the question can

22
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:36,650
machines think, which he considered too
meaningless to deserve discussion, with a

23
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:41,960
game. An interrogator converses by text
with a hidden human and a hidden machine.

24
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:46,610
If the interrogator cannot reliably tell
which is which, the machine has done

25
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:48,440
everything we could reasonably ask.

26
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:52,140
The imitation game is
operational, which is its virtue.

27
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,900
It is also a test of human-likeness,
which is its weakness.

28
00:01:56,220 --> 00:02:01,730
In two thousand twenty five, Cameron Jones
and Benjamin Bergen ran a pre-registered

29
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:04,520
version with Turing's
original three-party design:

30
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:09,550
judges chatted for five minutes with a
human and a machine at the same time, then

31
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:10,830
picked which one was human.

32
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:17,170
GPT-4.5, prompted to adopt a particular
humanlike persona, was judged to be the

33
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:21,240
human seventy three percent of the time,
more often than the real humans were.

34
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:26,480
A Meta model given the same persona prompt
was picked fifty six percent of the time.

35
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:33,070
Baseline systems, GPT-4o and the nineteen
sixties chatbot ELIZA, were picked only

36
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:34,380
about a fifth of the time.

37
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:39,610
So both of the systems that passed were
playing a character someone had written for

38
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:44,630
them. That's the lesson. What the test
measured was how well a system could play a

39
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:49,640
particular kind of human. That's a real
capability, and a socially important one,

40
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:53,000
given what it implies about who
you might be talking to online.

41
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:55,600
But it is not a definition
of intelligence.

42
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:59,260
The deeper story starts
with Claude Shannon.

43
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:04,060
In his nineteen forty eight theory of
communication, information is surprise:

44
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:08,800
an event with probability p
carries minus log two of p bits.

45
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:13,910
In a nineteen fifty one paper, Shannon
estimated the information content of English

46
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:18,520
by asking people to guess the next
letter of a text, one letter at a time.

47
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:22,950
People were good at it, because they
understood the language and the world the text

48
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,450
described. Shannon was using people's
knowledge of the language as a measuring

49
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:32,280
instrument: the better they guessed, the
less information each new letter carried.

50
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:37,850
Here's the key link. A technique called
arithmetic coding can encode a symbol that a

51
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:41,940
model assigned probability p using
very nearly minus log two of p bits.

52
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:46,430
So the better your model predicts the next
symbol, the fewer bits you need to encode

53
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:49,580
it. Prediction and compression
are the same problem.

54
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:54,730
Train a model to minimize prediction error,
measured as cross-entropy, and you are

55
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:56,340
literally training it to compress.

56
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:01,690
Which is why language model loss is
reported in bits or nats per token.

57
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:04,760
Pretraining is compression
with extra steps.

58
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:09,970
And researchers at DeepMind made that
literal in a two thousand twenty three paper

59
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:12,520
titled Language Modeling Is Compression.

60
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:18,330
They used a large model trained mostly on
text as a general-purpose compressor, and

61
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,880
it squeezed image patches and audio smaller
than the standard specialized formats,

62
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:26,220
PNG and FLAC. The caveat matters:

63
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:31,390
count the size of the model itself and
it loses badly, because the model is

64
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:36,320
enormous. But the result shows how much
general structure a good predictor absorbs.

65
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:41,840
If good prediction means good compression,
what is the best possible predictor?

66
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:46,160
In the nineteen sixties, Ray
Solomonoff gave a remarkable answer.

67
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:50,290
Consider every computer program that could
have produced the data you've seen so

68
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:55,350
far. Weight each program by two to the
minus its length in bits, so that shorter

69
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:57,040
programs count for much more.

70
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:02,110
Then predict the next observation by
averaging over what all those programs would

71
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:05,880
output next. This is Occam's
razor made mathematical.

72
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:10,630
The length of the shortest program that
produces a given string is called its

73
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:15,610
Kolmogorov complexity, after Andrei
Kolmogorov, who developed the idea

74
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:18,210
independently, as did Gregory Chaitin.

75
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:23,150
Solomonoff showed that if the data come
from any computable process at all, his

76
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:26,890
predictor's total error is bounded
by the complexity of that process.

77
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:32,350
It learns any computable world, and no
computable predictor can beat it by more than

78
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:35,640
a constant. The catch is
that it is uncomputable.

79
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:40,850
Evaluating it would require knowing which
programs ever halt, which is impossible in

80
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:44,500
general. So it is a gold
standard, not an algorithm.

81
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:48,820
But it gives a precise sense in which
intelligence and compression are linked:

82
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:53,580
the best predictor is the one that finds
the shortest explanation of the data.

83
00:05:54,440 --> 00:06:00,150
In two thousand seven, Shane Legg, who
would later co-found DeepMind, and Marcus

84
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:03,520
Hutter used this machinery to
define intelligence itself.

85
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:09,270
They first gathered around seventy
informal definitions from psychologists, AI

86
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:12,680
researchers and dictionaries,
and distilled their common core:

87
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:17,610
intelligence measures an agent's ability
to achieve goals in a wide range of

88
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:20,100
environments. Then they formalized it.

89
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:25,030
Imagine every computable environment
an agent could face, each one providing

90
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:30,140
observations and rewards. Measure the
reward the agent can expect to earn in each.

91
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:35,130
Then take a weighted sum over all of them,
weighting each environment by two to the

92
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:39,760
minus its Kolmogorov complexity, so
that simpler environments count more.

93
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,860
That sum is the agent's
universal intelligence.

94
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:48,670
Hutter had earlier defined an agent called
AIXI that maximizes exactly this kind of

95
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:52,320
quantity, by combining Solomonoff's
predictor with planning:

96
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:58,110
consider every program consistent with what
you've seen, weighted by simplicity, and

97
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:01,820
choose the action with the best
expected outcome across all of them.

98
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:06,120
In that precise sense, AIXI is the
most intelligent possible agent.

99
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:10,160
It is also uncomputable.
Approximations exist.

100
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:15,976
In two thousand eleven, a team led by Joel
Veness built one and tested it on a set

101
00:07:16,026 --> 00:07:20,850
of small problems, the largest a version
of Pac-Man in which the agent could sense

102
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:22,600
only its immediate surroundings.

103
00:07:22,940 --> 00:07:25,480
But there's a known
problem with all of this.

104
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:30,430
The answer depends on which universal
Turing machine you use to measure program

105
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:33,470
length. Yes, and it is not a technicality.

106
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:38,230
Kolmogorov complexity is only defined
up to a constant that depends on that

107
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:43,450
reference machine. In two thousand fifteen,
Jan Leike and Hutter showed how much it

108
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:48,700
matters. With an unlucky choice of reference
machine, AIXI can behave very badly,

109
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:52,820
and Legg and Hutter's measure
becomes, in their word, subjective.

110
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:58,430
Worse, by one natural standard of
optimality, every possible policy counts as

111
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:03,370
optimal across the class of all computable
environments, which makes that standard

112
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:09,450
empty. Universal intelligence is universal
only relative to a choice of what counts

113
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:15,050
as simple. That connects to one of the
deepest results in this area, the No Free

114
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:19,830
Lunch theorems, proved by David Wolpert
in the mid nineteen nineties, for learning

115
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:22,980
on his own and for optimization
with William Macready.

116
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:28,490
Averaged over all possible problems, every
learning or optimization method performs

117
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:30,340
exactly as well as every other.

118
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:35,280
Averaged across every conceivable
world, no method beats random guessing.

119
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:39,660
So there's no such thing as general
intelligence in the absolute.

120
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:44,630
Not in the absolute. An intelligence is
good across a particular distribution of

121
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:48,470
problems, and it gets there by having
assumptions, priors, that fit that

122
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:52,780
distribution. Legg and Hutter's simplicity
weighting is one such assumption:

123
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:54,680
that the world tends to be simple.

124
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:59,750
Evolution built different assumptions into
crows, octopuses and us, fitted to our

125
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:04,010
different worlds. So whenever someone
calls a system generally intelligent, the

126
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:05,620
right follow-up question is:

127
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:12,140
general across what? In two thousand
nineteen, François Chollet, then at Google,

128
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:17,010
published a long paper called On the Measure
of Intelligence that builds on exactly

129
00:09:17,060 --> 00:09:20,920
that point. His first move is to
separate skill from intelligence.

130
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:23,980
Skill at a task can be bought in two ways:

131
00:09:24,120 --> 00:09:28,990
by building in prior knowledge, or by
training on vast amounts of task-specific

132
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:34,570
experience. A chess engine is enormously
skilled at chess, and that tells you little

133
00:09:34,620 --> 00:09:38,950
about its intelligence. So Chollet
defines intelligence as skill-acquisition

134
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,750
efficiency: how efficiently a system turns
its priors and its experience into skill

135
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:49,270
on new tasks, weighted by how hard
those tasks are to generalize to.

136
00:09:49,320 --> 00:09:51,300
That has a pointed consequence.

137
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:56,910
Two systems with equal skill can differ
greatly in intelligence if one needed far

138
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:02,130
more data. On Chollet's measure, a system
that learned from trillions of words what

139
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:06,530
a child learns from millions is showing
a great deal of skill and not much

140
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:11,650
efficiency. To compare fairly with people,
Chollet proposed controlling for priors,

141
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:16,150
and he turned to the developmental
psychologist Elizabeth Spelke's work on core

142
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:21,090
knowledge: a small set of systems that
infants seem to have very early, for objects,

143
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:24,940
for agents and their goals,
for number, and for geometry.

144
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:30,450
A fair test of general intelligence
should require only those priors, plus the

145
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:32,720
ability to learn. So he built one:

146
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:35,560
the Abstraction and
Reasoning Corpus, or ARC.

147
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:40,910
Each task shows a few examples of colored
grids being transformed, and asks for the

148
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:45,840
output on a new input. Every task follows
a different rule, designed to be new.

149
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:49,420
Most people solve most of them,
usually within a few minutes.

150
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:56,480
For years, AI systems did terribly on
ARC, even as they aced standardized exams.

151
00:10:57,200 --> 00:11:01,710
Competitions in two thousand twenty and
two thousand twenty four produced steady

152
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:06,510
progress through program search and test-time
training, in which a model is briefly

153
00:11:06,560 --> 00:11:09,020
fine-tuned on each task's own examples.

154
00:11:09,380 --> 00:11:15,450
Then, in December two thousand twenty four,
a preview of OpenAI's o3 reasoning model

155
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:21,190
scored about seventy six percent on a
held-out set, using six samples per task at

156
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:23,220
roughly twenty six dollars a task.

157
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:28,030
With over a thousand samples per task and
around a hundred and seventy times the

158
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:30,930
computing cost, it reached
about eighty eight percent.

159
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:33,700
Two caveats belong with those numbers.

160
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,540
The tested model had been trained on
three-quarters of ARC's public training set,

161
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:42,320
and the model OpenAI later
released was a different version.

162
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:48,082
A second, harder version, ARC-AGI-2, launched
in March two thousand twenty five, and

163
00:11:48,132 --> 00:11:50,940
pure language models scored
essentially zero on it.

164
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:56,850
At the end of that year's competition, the
winning open entry, called NVARC, scored

165
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:59,780
about twenty four percent at
around twenty cents a task.

166
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:05,517
The best verified commercial model was
Claude Opus 4.5, at about thirty eight

167
00:12:05,567 --> 00:12:09,890
percent. That is a model from Anthropic,
the company that makes the model writing

168
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:14,770
this series. A refinement harness built
by a company called Poetiq, wrapped around

169
00:12:14,820 --> 00:12:19,720
Google's Gemini 3 Pro, reached fifty four
percent at about thirty dollars a task.

170
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:24,950
And the competition's paper award went to
a tiny recursive model, with seven million

171
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,450
parameters and two layers, which scored
forty five percent on the first version of

172
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:31,390
ARC and eight percent on the second.

173
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:37,330
Seven million parameters, beating several
far larger language models from earlier

174
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:40,940
that year. That says something
about what scale is buying.

175
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:45,640
It says that on this narrow kind of task,
a small network with the right structure,

176
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:49,740
trained on the puzzles themselves,
can outdo much larger general models.

177
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:55,530
Then came ARC-AGI-3, launched in March
two thousand twenty six, which abandons

178
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:58,540
static puzzles for interactive
games with no instructions.

179
00:12:59,020 --> 00:13:04,530
A system has to explore, work out what the
game is, and win, and it is scored on how

180
00:13:04,580 --> 00:13:09,370
efficiently it acts. At launch, human
testers solved all of the games, and frontier

181
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:11,970
AI systems scored around
half of one percent.

182
00:13:12,020 --> 00:13:15,260
Meanwhile the older test
was nearly saturated:

183
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:21,870
by July, ARC's verified results put Claude
Opus 5, another Anthropic model, at about

184
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:26,440
ninety percent on ARC-AGI-2, and
about thirty percent on the new games.

185
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:32,817
In September two thousand twenty six,
OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra scored about sixty three

186
00:13:32,867 --> 00:13:37,050
percent under ARC's standard test harness,
and nearly one hundred percent under a

187
00:13:37,100 --> 00:13:39,840
different harness, a
so-called provider adapter.

188
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:44,690
Even with the reasoning setting matched,
it was about sixty three against ninety

189
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:49,290
nine. The ARC Prize organizers were
explicit that this was not AGI.

190
00:13:49,340 --> 00:13:52,330
Sixty three versus a hundred.

191
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:56,580
Same model, same games. Whose
intelligence is being measured:

192
00:13:56,800 --> 00:14:00,200
the model's, or the model plus
whatever was built around it?

193
00:14:00,420 --> 00:14:03,440
That's exactly the question,
and it generalizes.

194
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:09,690
Tools, retries, memory, prompting strategies
and hand-built harnesses can change a

195
00:14:09,740 --> 00:14:13,680
score dramatically. Which brings us
to the science of measurement itself.

196
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:19,850
Measuring AI capability has become its
own discipline, and it is harder than it

197
00:14:19,900 --> 00:14:22,140
looks. Benchmarks saturate:

198
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:26,520
a test that once separated systems
ends up with everyone near the ceiling.

199
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:32,030
Benchmarks leak: test questions find their
way into training data, so models can

200
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:35,190
score well partly by memory,
a modern Clever Hans.

201
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:40,010
When a team led by Hugh Zhang wrote a
fresh set of grade-school math problems

202
00:14:40,060 --> 00:14:45,020
matched to a popular benchmark, some
models' accuracy dropped by up to eight

203
00:14:45,070 --> 00:14:49,770
percent, and the size of the drop tracked
how readily a model could reproduce the

204
00:14:49,820 --> 00:14:54,850
original test items. Many models, especially
at the frontier, showed little sign of

205
00:14:54,900 --> 00:14:57,100
overfitting, which is worth saying too.

206
00:14:57,360 --> 00:15:02,170
And benchmarks invite optimization,
which triggers Goodhart's law, in the

207
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:05,320
anthropologist Marilyn
Strathern's well-known phrasing:

208
00:15:05,580 --> 00:15:09,020
when a measure becomes a target,
it ceases to be a good measure.

209
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:12,860
There's a deeper problem, one
that episode one would recognize:

210
00:15:13,380 --> 00:15:19,170
construct validity. In two thousand twenty
five, a team led by Andrew Bean at Oxford

211
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:22,780
reviewed four hundred and forty
five language model benchmarks.

212
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:27,850
Only about sixteen percent used statistical
tests to compare models, and roughly

213
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:32,770
half set out to measure an abstract
concept, like reasoning or safety, without

214
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:37,630
clearly defining it. Psychometrics spent
a century learning to ask what a test

215
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:40,920
measures, and whether it measures
the same thing across groups.

216
00:15:41,420 --> 00:15:44,580
Much of AI evaluation is
starting that work now.

217
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:48,720
Researchers have also tried
to define the target itself.

218
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:53,100
One approach defines general
intelligence by comparison with people.

219
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:58,330
In October two thousand twenty five, thirty
three researchers led by Dan Hendrycks

220
00:15:58,380 --> 00:16:03,870
proposed defining AGI as matching the
cognitive versatility and proficiency of a

221
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:09,070
well-educated adult, across ten domains
drawn from the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model we

222
00:16:09,120 --> 00:16:11,500
met in episode one, weighted equally.

223
00:16:11,760 --> 00:16:17,803
They scored GPT-4 at about twenty seven
percent and GPT-5 at about fifty seven

224
00:16:17,853 --> 00:16:22,930
percent, with long-term memory storage
the weakest area, where both models scored

225
00:16:22,980 --> 00:16:28,190
zero. One natural objection is that this
measures how human-like a profile of

226
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:31,620
abilities is, which may not be
the same thing as intelligence.

227
00:16:32,020 --> 00:16:34,600
A different approach
measures autonomy directly.

228
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:39,710
The research group METR estimates the
length of tasks, measured by how long they

229
00:16:39,760 --> 00:16:44,530
take skilled people, that AI agents can
complete with fifty percent reliability.

230
00:16:44,580 --> 00:16:49,797
By their estimates, that horizon doubled
roughly every seven months between two

231
00:16:49,847 --> 00:16:54,290
thousand nineteen and two thousand twenty
five, and closer to every four months

232
00:16:54,340 --> 00:16:55,850
since two thousand twenty three.

233
00:16:55,900 --> 00:17:00,650
The most capable systems measured in two
thousand twenty six are put at more than a

234
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:05,970
working day, but METR itself warns that
measurements above about sixteen hours are

235
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:08,560
unreliable with its
current set of test tasks.

236
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:13,000
And a third approach borrows the
psychometrics of episode one directly.

237
00:17:14,580 --> 00:17:18,350
In two thousand twenty three, David Ilić
analyzed more than twelve hundred language

238
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:23,143
models on a public leaderboard and found
that a single factor explained about eighty

239
00:17:23,193 --> 00:17:25,960
five percent of the variation
in their benchmark scores:

240
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:28,720
a strong machine positive manifold.

241
00:17:29,100 --> 00:17:34,650
In April two thousand twenty six, the
complexity scientist David Krakauer asked how

242
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:40,500
that structure changes over time, in a
paper titled The Rise and Fall of G in AGI.

243
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:45,370
Across thirty nine models, on the eight
most widely reported benchmarks, scores

244
00:17:45,420 --> 00:17:50,030
correlated strongly, around point eight
on average, and on one core battery the

245
00:17:50,080 --> 00:17:53,840
first factor explained about ninety two
percent of the variation in two thousand

246
00:17:53,890 --> 00:17:56,770
twenty three and early
two thousand twenty four.

247
00:17:56,820 --> 00:18:00,610
It had begun to slip by mid two
thousand twenty four, and after

248
00:18:00,660 --> 00:18:04,750
reasoning-specialized models arrived
it fell to about sixty four percent.

249
00:18:04,800 --> 00:18:09,430
The data are thin. Less than half of the
model and benchmark combinations were

250
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:12,900
actually measured, and the latest
period has only four models.

251
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:14,840
So treat it as a suggestion.

252
00:18:15,080 --> 00:18:17,980
But it fits the common-cause
story from episode one.

253
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:21,960
When scale was the main thing
changing, everything rose together.

254
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:26,930
As labs began training for particular
skills, the general factor weakened within

255
00:18:26,980 --> 00:18:31,110
each new group of models, though pooled
across all of them it still accounts for

256
00:18:31,160 --> 00:18:32,870
about ninety percent of the variation.

257
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:38,930
Which would make machine g an artifact of
how the systems were built, not a property

258
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:43,020
of intelligence. Or a property
of one era of building them.

259
00:18:43,340 --> 00:18:48,350
Either way, the structure of machine
abilities has shifted within a few years, far

260
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:50,360
faster than anything measured in people.

261
00:18:51,460 --> 00:18:54,360
So, after all of this,
do we have a definition?

262
00:18:54,780 --> 00:18:57,910
We have several, and they
disagree in revealing ways.

263
00:18:57,960 --> 00:19:03,000
The psychometric view defines intelligence
by how abilities covary in people.

264
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:08,630
The algorithmic view of Solomonoff, Legg
and Hutter defines it as expected success

265
00:19:08,680 --> 00:19:11,760
across all environments,
weighted by simplicity:

266
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:16,410
elegant, uncomputable, and relative
to a choice of reference machine.

267
00:19:16,460 --> 00:19:20,470
Chollet defines it as efficiency
of learning relative to priors.

268
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:25,950
And the practical views define it by what
systems can actually do, how long they can

269
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,140
work on their own, and how
human-like their profile is.

270
00:19:29,400 --> 00:19:32,680
For large language models,
these come apart sharply.

271
00:19:33,160 --> 00:19:37,990
Their huge training sets count heavily
against them on Chollet's measure, and not at

272
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:43,370
all on Legg and Hutter's. Their uneven
profile shows up immediately on Hendrycks's.

273
00:19:43,420 --> 00:19:47,620
And their scores depend on harnesses in
ways that make every number contestable.

274
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:51,930
What the definitions share is a core idea
that will run through the rest of this

275
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:56,890
series: intelligence is bound up with
prediction, with building models good enough

276
00:19:56,940 --> 00:19:58,660
to anticipate what comes next.

277
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:02,280
Legg and Hutter haven't stopped
at intelligence, either.

278
00:20:02,740 --> 00:20:07,390
In September two thousand twenty six, both
were among the authors of a framework for

279
00:20:07,440 --> 00:20:11,790
assessing whether AI systems might be
conscious, which we'll examine in episode

280
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:15,730
twelve. But where does the drive to
predict come from in the first place?

281
00:20:15,780 --> 00:20:21,790
Next episode, we go back to the origin of
life, and to the idea that life itself is

282
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:22,840
computation.
